00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.939 align:start position:0%
 
now this morning a teenager is fighting  for his life in hospital after being  stabbed in the chest on a bus in North  London yesterday afternoon just the  latest victim in what some are  describing as a national emergency so is  enough being done to tackle knife crime  well joining us now is the health  secretary thank you very much for being  with us on the program this morning  you've written in The Sunday Telegraph  today about the role the NHS can play in  trying to help tackle knife crime so  what are you proposing well I think all  of us touched and moved by these awful  stories of these these attacks these  murders due to knife crime and I think  lots of us look at the children who are  involved and the young adults and think  they could be our children and this is  one of the reasons why it's really hit a  nerve with so many people  the truth is with knife crime you can  only tackle it with lots of different  people come together it isn't just an  issue for the police although that  obviously they're very important and so  I want the NHS to play its full part in  making sure that we rise to this  challenge that everybody feels is so  important to tackle and not least  because when these young people when  these children arrive in hospital that  is a moment when you can intervene and  try to tackle the problem and you know  they were just little kids just a few  years ago and the idea that this can  only be tackled with the strong arm of  the law is is wrong when they arrived in  hospital that's a moment that you can  really make a strong intervention and  get people involved to try to turn  people's lives around at the same time  now NHS staff are already under immense  time pressure we know about the  challenges in the NHS as well document  it I mean is this really just trying to  put a sticking-plaster if you like over  the fact that so many police have been  cut around 20,000 since 2010 and trying  to get NHS workers to plug that gap no  it's about working across the  different silos of government if you  like this approach has been tried in  Baltimore which has in the past been one  of the most violent cities in the world  and and it's worked very effectively to  reduce by around 85% the number of  people who come back with another knife  injury and it's about getting the right  intervention to these to these children  to these young people at the right  moment and often the moment that you  arrive in hospital is a it is obviously  a critical moment for anybody and that's  when people are open to changing their  behavior and to responding differently  and if you just treat the physical  person then you're only treating part of  the problem because what we don't want  to do is just patch people up and send  them back out into exactly the same  environment that led them in in the  first place  what we want to do is at that moment  intervene to give them the full support  to turn around to get out of the gangs  and to and to make the changes needed to  get away from knife crime okay now we do  of course need to talk about brexit the  voter's looming next week already it  feels as if the mood music it's pretty  bleak around Westminster so is it  inevitable that the Prime Minister is  going to lose the vote on Tuesday no  it's not inevitable it's in the gift of  MPs to get on and deliver on brexit and  I very much hope that's what people will  vote for because that is what the public  voted for almost three years ago now we  have the opportunity on Tuesday to be  able to deliver on the referendum I'll  be voting to deliver on the results of  the referendum and I very much hope that  all of my colleagues will too and at the  same time they of course that people are  looking to what will happen if that vote  doesn't go through Theresa May has  promised MPs will get to decide whether  or not that would mean leaving with no  deal on the 29th of March or extending  article 50 so can you just confirm if  those votes are deaf  gonna happen this week and if they are  how you would vote on the No Deal  decision  well the Prime Minister has said said  that absolutely but I think the truth is  that in the event of this vote on  Tuesday not going through there would  nobody knows what would happen in in the  in everything that followed there would  be total uncertainty that would be the  only certainty and so I would appeal  very directly to all of my colleagues  from right across the party and  supporters in other parties people who  believe that brexit should happen  believe we should honor the result of  the referendum I would appeal to every  single one of them to vote for this deal  whether or not they they like any of the  individual details Benjamin Franklin  said of the US Constitution I vote for  this with all its faults and I think  that's the approach that we need to take  on Tuesday is to get together and vote  to deliver on the result and then we  won't have to have any votes afterwards  on on what happens next we can get on  because there's so many opportunities in  the future there's so many opportunities  that we can seize if we can deliver on  the brexit result and and then help  shape Britain in the 21st century in  what we can achieve post brexit instead  of constantly having this fog of brexit  descended over our politics as it has  been over the last few months so hang on  it just to be really clear are you  saying then that there is no guarantee  that if the government loses the vote on  Tuesday that MPs will then get to decide  whether or not to leave with no deal or  to extend article 50 no I'm not saying  that at all that's what the Prime  Minister has committed to I was making  the much broader point that we wouldn't  know what would happen in those votes we  wouldn't know what the EU would then  come back with in many ways that you  believes that it would be in the driving  seat in that if this vote doesn't go  through which is not where anybody wants  to  certainly not where I want to be the  point I was making was not about the  individual votes on Wednesday or  Thursday the point is about the the  broader politics of uncertainty that  we'd have if this vote doesn't go  through and that's why I'm going to vote  for it and it's why I think I just I  appeal directly to all of my colleagues  to vote for it whatever the whatever the  details because it's so important that  we deliver on the result of the  referendum and it's so important that we  can then get on to all the amazing  things this country can do and all the  other things that we want to be talking  about ok well that's certainly coming  through loud and clear that you're gonna  be voting in support with the prime  minister's deal would you what would you  vote for though because I know that you  want to focus of course on the vote on  Tuesday but I think it's not an  unreasonable question to ask after last  time the prime minister suffered the  biggest defeat of any sitting government  when she put the deal to the house would  you vote to leave with no deal or to  extend article 15 well I don't want  those votes to happen and I'm not going  to talk about how I'm going to vote in  them ahead of time these negotiations  are still ongoing  you know there's still been this weekend  have been negotiations going on in  Brussels to try to get more out of the  EU that to provide assurances in order  to get everybody behind winning that  vote on Tuesday so I I think that's  absolutely as you say what we need to  concentrate on what happens after that  if the vote goes the wrong way is is  clearly a huge uncertainty but I'm not  going to go into how I'm going to vote  on any of those on only have votes until  until those votes are called and I very  much hope that they won't be and there's  a way that we can all avoid having those  votes called and that's motive of the  deal on Tuesday you're talking there  about the uncertainty that would happen  if the deal doesn't go through of course  the NHS is one of the institutions that  would be very very impacted by whatever  happens when it comes to a brexit so  would there be more money for the NHS if  the Prime Minister still go through  well yes the 20 billion pounds extra  that we're putting into the NHS is  happening in any brexit scenario that's  committed by the whole government that's  very important and and you know talking  about how we spend that money and what  we want from the NHS in the future  building on the long term plan that we  published earlier this year these are  the sorts of things that I think we  should be talking about they're the  sorts of things that we need to be  working on to deliver for the people who  we serve delivering on the referendum  result is a first step towards getting  back to talking about the  bread-and-butter issues that people  really care about on the doorstep so the  the you know the the details of that  plan what we need to do to make sure  that people take more responsibility for  their own health as well the the the  prevention agenda and of course dealing  with issues like knife crime that we  were talking about at the start these  are all the sorts of things that I want  our politics to get back to talking  about because they are the things that  really matter for improving this country  and delivering on delivering on brexit  and doing it you know taking the deal  that's on the table and delivering on  that is a it's a critical first step to  be able to making that progress the  world college of radiologists warren  recently that hospitals will have no  choice but to prioritize which patients  receive cancer treatment if there's no  deal so is that a description that you  recognize will there some people maybe  have delays their cancer treatment if  there's no deal no I heard those remarks  and they're wrong we have plans in place  to ensure that we can get all of the  drugs into the country in in in in  whatever the brexit scenario that  includes the very short shelf-life  isotopes that are needed for for  treating cancer and so there are there  are plans in place to make sure that all  of that can happen we've been working  very hard on making sure that the NHS is  there in also  Constance's in whatever the brexit vote  and of course you know in a way the NHS  is designed for dealing with problems  with with you know with individual  crises in individual people's lives and  more broadly we prepare for all sorts of  eventualities in the NHS and I would say  that the NHS has really risen to the  challenge of the concerns about No Deal  brexit in the event that the were very  serious blockages at the border you know  there's 12,300 medicines that we use in  England at any one time and we've now  got a plan for how those medicines can  come through even if there's blockages  at the border so this is something we've  been doing a lot of work on to make sure  and that it can happen and now we're  confident that if everybody does what  they need to do then that supply of  medicines will continue unhindered now  just finally before you go when I was  researching this interview I couldn't  help but notice how unbelievably  competitive you seem to be so in 2005  you got frostbite after trying to play  the most northerly game of cricket ever  in the Arctic in 2012 you trained as a  jockey and then actually won a race that  Newmarket  you entered Parma in 2010 now your house  secretary so are you so competitive but  now you've got your eye on the top job  could you be persuaded no I could see  where this question was going right from  the start no I think that I think that  politics and in particular in government  it's a real it's a team effort  I love being health secretary I feel  like I can make a contribution and I  think that it's really important that we  work together I mean this is one of the  reasons why I feel on brexit that we  need to come together for this vote on  Tuesday but more broadly I see it a  great privilege to serve in government  and in the cabinet and I think of the  you know the millions of people who rely  on the services that I'm responsible for  every day and I think it's a good team  effort you know people ask me why  you doubt - it's a it's a team effort  and I thoroughly enjoyed being part of  that team

